{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And its height five.</b> This implies that this was the height of the hangings. And if you ask, according to the one who says the altar was ten cubits high, if so, [the priest] would be seen [from outside] — the priest and the service in his hand. One answers that the height [of five] is from the edge of the altar and above, making it fifteen cubits. And the Rav, Rabbi Yitzhak bar Avraham, raised a different difficulty: if they were no higher than five cubits, then the gate opening was not as high as the opening of the Hekhal of the eternal House. Therefore he would explain: certainly the hangings of the north and south and west were fifteen cubits high, and this 'and its height' refers to the hangings on the east, which had a height of five cubits more than the hangings of the other directions — if so, their height was twenty, and the opening's height was twenty as well."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:04:11.692034367Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}